WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:13.250
Dear friends of VISUALPOLITIK, I’m sure
I don’t have to do much to convince you

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:22.150
that Latin America is one of the most unsafe
and volatile regions in the world.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:30.160
Unfortunately, violence and crime have made
some Latin American areas very dangerous.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:34.660
As you may know, we’re talking about a region
that has been strongly infested with drug

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:39.510
trafficking, arms trafficking and many other
illegal activities, which have been executed

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:44.269
without the individual states having been
able to – or let’s not fool ourselves,

00:00:44.269 --> 00:00:51.030
wanting to, – fix the situation.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:54.830
Even though it may be hard to understand,
many criminal groups have built extremely

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:58.369
close links with governments and public institutions
in the region.

00:00:58.369 --> 00:01:04.800
In some cases, these groups are even able
to influence, control and direct entire governments.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:10.350
Here on VisualPolitik, we recently talked
about the case of Central America.

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:14.730
But, there’s another volatile country that
we can’t ignore and it’s one which has

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:21.180
shot to the lead in recent years and may yet
become the most dangerous country on the planet:

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:24.260
I am, of course, talking about Nicolás Maduro’s
Venezuela.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:30.260
[TEXT: Venezuela’s saving grace is its popular
power.

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:37.020
And popular power must work fully and for
all.]

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:38.050
This is not a new story.

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:43.040
We’ve already talked on this channel about
how Venezuela is becoming a narco-dictatorship

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:44.560
at breakneck speed.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:47.900
But, unfortunately, that’s not the worst
of it.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:52.520
In today's Venezuela, crime and organized
crime are so much a part of the scenery that

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:57.320
if crime decreases, it’s not outlandish
to conclude that there are simply fewer and

00:01:57.320 --> 00:01:59.310
fewer things to steal.

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:00.520
(CHART)

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:06.690
Almost 30,000 fatalities each year in a country
that’s not at war.

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:12.610
130,000 violent deaths in the last five years
alone, according to released data.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:17.740
That’s truly awful.

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:23.170
What’s strange is that, as you can see on
the screen, both the known homicide rates

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:28.000
and the number of violent deaths began to
decrease in 2017.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.020
Strange considering that this country is worse
than ever both economically and socially,

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:33.020
don't you think?

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:36.430
And I know, some of you may be thinking...
it’s probably because of all the emigration

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:38.670
Venezuela has experienced recently.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:43.930
More than four million people have left, hundreds
and hundreds of thousands, millions of young

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:47.350
people have emigrated, which has to have an
effect on the statistics.

00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:52.069
[TEXT: Because those of us who’ve had to
leave practically in exile, we want justice

00:02:52.069 --> 00:02:57.370
to return to Venezuela and freedom.]

00:02:57.370 --> 00:02:58.690
Well yes... and no.

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:00.020
It’s a truly grey area.

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:03.560
In this video we’re going to talk about
the effects of crime in Venezuela.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:08.410
We’re talking about institutionalized crime,
which has largely been caused by the government

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:09.410
itself.

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:14.720
And we’re also going to talk about how crime
has reached such high levels that it’s no

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:18.860
longer profitable for common criminals to
commit crimes within Venezuela, causing many

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:22.060
to seek better opportunities outside.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:25.220
Listen up.

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:27.650
(EVIL IN POWER)

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:31.170
First of all, we need to set the scene a little.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:35.000
Let’s say we’re in a regular country,
it’s not unusual for the government to fight

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:40.680
crime and focus resources on persecuting the
bad, right?

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:43.450
Well, in Venezuela it’s not like that.

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:45.830
Here we find the opposite: the bad guys govern.

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:46.980
Take a look.

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:52.349
(AUDIO: Tareck El Aissami: United States includes
Venezuelan Minister in the 10 most wanted

00:03:52.349 --> 00:03:54.550
list for international narcotraffic.

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:55.550
- BBC)

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:59.250
(AUDIO: Spanish judge investigates half of
Maduro’s government for money laundering

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:00.250
from oil.

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:01.250
- El Confidencial)

00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:02.250
Surprised?

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:05.670
I bet you aren’t, especially if you’ve
been following VisualPolitik for a while.

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:09.569
You’ve probably seen our video on how Venezuela
has become a narco-state.

00:04:09.569 --> 00:04:13.800
And in case you haven’t, I’ll leave the
link in the description.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:20.130
The truth is that there’s not much that
can surprise us anymore from Venezuela.

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:21.379
Except for maybe one thing..:

00:04:21.379 --> 00:04:25.580
How the Chavista regime has managed to monopolize
street crime.

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:29.990
(AUDIO: Maduro calls for ideologically related
groups to “defend peace”.

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:30.990
- El Universal)

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:32.360
Yes, that’s right.

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:37.319
The Venezuelan government has literally thousands
of paid criminals.

00:04:37.319 --> 00:04:42.710
Criminals who in many cases have been armed
by the State itself to function as parapolitical

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:44.580
groups for political repression.

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:47.919
They’re known as “collectives”.

00:04:47.919 --> 00:04:54.330
The collectives are groups that mobilize at
the government’s request, especially when

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:55.780
the opposition protests.

00:04:55.780 --> 00:05:00.889
Then they move around on motorcycles carrying
long weapons and shooting at everything that

00:05:00.889 --> 00:05:05.680
moves, with absolute impunity, while the police
and the Bolivarian National Guard watch them

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:06.680
impassively.

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:09.490
In many cases, they even act together.

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:16.289
In other words, that’s how Venezuela’s
violence has been controlled by the State,

00:05:16.289 --> 00:05:21.169
not for the purpose of ending it, but to dominate
and mobilize it against opposing citizens.

00:05:21.169 --> 00:05:24.610
And no, that’s not the end of it.

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:28.779
In addition to the deadly collectives – which
is how they’re popularly known – the actual

00:05:28.779 --> 00:05:34.380
state security forces – well so-called security
– act as true criminals.

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:44.319
To give you a better idea, according to the
data provided by the UN, almost 7,000 people

00:05:44.319 --> 00:05:51.240
lost their lives due to “resistance to authority”
between January 2018 and May 2019, that is,

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.349
in just over a year there were 7,000 extrajudicial
executions.

00:05:53.349 --> 00:05:56.800
(AUDIO: Policemen and criminals share crimes
together in Venezuela.

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:57.919
- La Razón)

00:05:57.919 --> 00:06:02.399
(AUDIO: 464 policemen are prosecuted in Venezuela
for extortion and kidnappings.

00:06:02.399 --> 00:06:03.399
- El Comercio)

00:06:03.399 --> 00:06:06.339
Basically, in Venezuela crime occurs at every
level.

00:06:06.339 --> 00:06:09.449
And that largely explains why it goes unpunished
as well.

00:06:09.449 --> 00:06:13.110
When the police themselves are often complicit...
it isn’t good news.

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:16.470
(AUDIO: 92% of homicides in Venezuela go unpunished.

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:17.470
- El Nacional)

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:19.840
And we’re not just saying that on this channel.

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:23.860
Even Michelle Bachelet, the UN High Commissioner
for Human Rights, described the situation

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:25.849
in her report on Venezuela.

00:06:25.849 --> 00:06:32.009
(AUDIO: “My office has continued to document
possible extrajudicial execution cases committed

00:06:32.009 --> 00:06:35.909
by members of the Special Police Forces, of
the National Police (FAES).

00:06:35.909 --> 00:06:41.750
Only last July the non-governmental organization
“Victim Monitor” identified 57 new cases

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:47.210
of alleged executions committed by FAES members
in Caracas” Michelle Bachelet)

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:51.879
And I don't think you can say, even though
Nicolás Maduro often makes such claims, that

00:06:51.879 --> 00:06:57.099
Bachelet is part of some international movement
that is hell-bent on persecuting the Bolivarian

00:06:57.099 --> 00:06:58.099
regime.

00:06:58.099 --> 00:07:03.749
Given Bachelet’s leftist leanings, it’d
be absurd to think so.

00:07:03.749 --> 00:07:10.139
And considering Venezuela’s situation, the
testimonies and information documented by

00:07:10.139 --> 00:07:14.560
the UN seem to be on the conservative side,
which isn’t all that surprising, by the

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:15.560
way.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:23.159
[TEXT: And we have to go into a definitive
battle in order to achieve peace in Venezuela.

00:07:23.159 --> 00:07:27.800
For a national unification.

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:31.610
Let's get ready to defeat them.]

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:32.610
And still there’s more.

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:35.860
Along with the collectives, and the state
security forces’ reprehensible actions,

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:41.819
the current situation in Venezuela has also
multiplied the incidence of small-scale crimes.

00:07:41.819 --> 00:07:46.719
Not to mention the widespread corruption that
reigns throughout the country.

00:07:46.719 --> 00:07:52.479
Nowadays in Venezuela, to get official documents,
passports or even to get medical attention,

00:07:52.479 --> 00:07:56.710
bribes are indispensable.

00:07:56.710 --> 00:07:59.270
And this has two fundamental reasons:

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:02.949
On the one hand we have poverty and practically
useless wages.

00:08:02.949 --> 00:08:05.550
Hyperinflation has made local salaries grossly
insufficient.

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:11.599
(AUDIO: The new minimum wage in Venezuela
is only enough to buy an egg carton.

00:08:11.599 --> 00:08:12.599
- VOA)

00:08:12.599 --> 00:08:16.409
And therefore petty crime becomes almost natural,
it's a matter of survival.

00:08:16.409 --> 00:08:22.219
When in need, people take advantage and try
to get any money they can, using the lack

00:08:22.219 --> 00:08:24.429
of control and impunity in their favor.

00:08:24.429 --> 00:08:26.319
Now, some of you may say… but Simon!

00:08:26.319 --> 00:08:28.610
Didn't you say before that crimes are decreasing?

00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:31.599
It doesn’t seem so with what you’ve just
described, right?

00:08:31.599 --> 00:08:37.320
Well, it may seem like a contradiction, but
the truth is that yes, icrime overall is decreasing,

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:39.300
and we’re going to tell you why.

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:42.700
Listen up because we have some curve balls
coming.

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:51.090
[The productive models we’ve tried so far
have failed.

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:59.480
And the responsibility is ours, it's mine,
it's yours.]

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:06.440
(WHY CRIME NO LONGER PAYS)
Basically it’s Venezuela’s sad but crude

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:07.440
reality.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:11.320
Things are so bad, that the crisis has even
affected criminals, or “malandros” as

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:12.670
they’re known in Venezuela.

00:09:12.670 --> 00:09:16.940
(AUDIO: Venezuela murder rate dips, partly
due to migration: monitoring group.

00:09:16.940 --> 00:09:17.940
- Reuters)

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:22.460
(AUDIO: Crisis in Venezuela reduces robberies
and assaults; criminals no longer can afford

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:23.460
bullets.- El Universal)

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:24.460
Yup.

00:09:24.460 --> 00:09:25.460
Think about it for a minute.

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:30.650
With a 200,000% inflation, which is what the
IMF forecasts for this year, what use is it

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:34.780
to steal small things if the value of the
money you’re going to get for them will

00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:38.050
deteriorate almost overnight?

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:42.700
What use is it to break into a bank's safe
if the bolivars inside are worthless?

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:47.080
Or what good is it to steal cars or motorcycles,
if you won’t be able to get spare parts

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:50.050
or afford the gas to use them?

00:09:50.050 --> 00:09:54.410
And if we add the emigration factor, well...
the equation is solved.

00:09:54.410 --> 00:10:00.730
Yes, it may seem surreal and hard to believe,
but the disaster that Nicolás Maduro is leading

00:10:00.730 --> 00:10:06.130
Venezuela into is causing thousands and thousands
of criminals to look for new places to commit

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:07.130
crimes.

00:10:07.130 --> 00:10:10.510
The result is that Venezuela is exporting
its crime.

00:10:10.510 --> 00:10:14.290
(AUDIO: Maduro boosts the exit of criminals
from Venezuela.

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:15.290
Panam Post)

00:10:15.290 --> 00:10:18.570
It’s true, Venezuela is so bad right now
that not even criminals want to stay.

00:10:18.570 --> 00:10:21.580
And do you know who the main victims of this
phenomenon are?

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:27.070
Yes, okay, the citizens of the countries unfortunate
to accept the criminals but, spare a thought

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:31.140
also for the other three million Venezuelans
who’ve emigrated.

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:36.510
Here on VisualPolitik we can’t avoid discussing
the effects that this phenomenon is having

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:39.680
on Venezuelan emigrants in the rest of Latin
America.

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:43.580
Because leaving your country behind, your
family, and on top of that everything you’ve

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:47.860
worked for is already traumatic enough, what
if what comes next is even more so?

00:10:47.860 --> 00:10:59.890
[TEXT: I didn’t want to leave my country,
I’m Venezuelan, I love Venezuela]

00:10:59.890 --> 00:11:04.940
(THE DIFFICULT CONDITION OF BEING VENEZUELAN
IN LATIN AMERICA)

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:10.990
That's right, for many, being Venezuelan has
become a challenge in some Latin American

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:11.990
countries.

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:15.920
Can you imagine what it’s like to have to
endure constant racist or xenophobic comments?

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:20.230
Or suffer abuse from the authorities just
because you come from a specific country?

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:26.930
Well, all this stems from Venezuelan criminals
leaving for neighboring countries.

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:31.150
This is how the lives of many Venezuelan emigrants
who do follow the rules – which is the vast

00:11:31.150 --> 00:11:33.920
majority – are becoming more difficult because
of the few.

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:36.450
And that causes situations like this:

00:11:36.450 --> 00:11:40.730
(AUDIO: Red tape and xenophobic attacks against
Venezuelans in Latin America.

00:11:40.730 --> 00:11:41.730
- France 24)

00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:45.290
In Peru, for example, we’ve recently seen
even congressmen, such as Esther Saavedra,

00:11:45.290 --> 00:11:48.010
harshly attacking Venezuelans from Congress
itself.

00:11:48.010 --> 00:11:55.060
[TEXT: “A million legal and illegal immigrants,
both workers and criminals, they have to eat,

00:11:55.060 --> 00:11:59.769
they have to sleep, they come and steal Peruvians’
jobs.

00:11:59.769 --> 00:12:04.000
(...) Venezuelans, good or bad, have to leave
Peru, leave”.]

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:09.360
Can you imagine running away from a dictatorship,
from hunger and violence, and receiving such

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:11.460
insults from public representatives?

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:17.900
It must be hard, don't you think?

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:22.790
Fortunately, this way of thinking doesn’t
represent all Peruvians, not at all, but hate

00:12:22.790 --> 00:12:26.670
speeches that pinpoint a specific nationality
are always worrying.

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:30.040
And coming from a country’s Congress, even
more so.

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:32.330
And that’s not the end of it...

00:12:32.330 --> 00:12:36.850
Even the Ecuadorian president, Lenín Moreno,
fell into the grip of this campaign after

00:12:36.850 --> 00:12:39.620
a pregnant woman was murdered by a Venezuelan.

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:44.670
(AUDIO: Ecuador announce the creation of brigades
to “control” Venezuelan immigrants.- Europa

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:45.670
Press)

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:48.660
Now, what is really behind this wave of hatred
against Venezuelans in Latin America?

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:54.960
Well, it has a lot to do with a poor migratory
culture.

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:59.230
Traditionally most of these countries haven’t
needed to receive migrants and suddenly, overnight,

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:03.850
there’s a Venezuelan exodus throughout the
whole continent.

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:08.090
Given that context, it doesn’t seem far-fetched
that many people perceive this movement with

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:12.110
fear, fear of something different, something
that comes from outside and the consequences

00:13:12.110 --> 00:13:13.110
that it may have.

00:13:13.110 --> 00:13:17.080
And this is even happening in Colombia:

00:13:17.080 --> 00:13:20.490
As Venezuela’s neighbor, it’s welcomed
the most Venezuelans, more than a million

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:25.760
and a half, since the great Venezuelan crisis
exploded between 2014 and 2015.

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:29.860
Its proximity and the fact that emigrating
from Venezuela to Colombia is cheaper, has

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:35.880
led many common Venezuelan criminals setting
themselves up in Colombia.

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:40.040
At first, the Colombian government’s attitude
aimed to help the Venezuelan population by

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:44.970
opening its borders, granting temporary permits
and accepting Venezuelans with expired passports.

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:50.090
However, this wave of solidarity at an institutional
level hasn’t always repeated at street level,

00:13:50.090 --> 00:13:55.040
in everyday life, since Colombia joins Peru,
Mexico, Chile and Ecuador among the countries

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:59.900
with the most cases of xenophobia reported
by Venezuelans.

00:13:59.900 --> 00:14:05.790
And when we talk about xenophobia, we mean
things like this:

00:14:05.790 --> 00:14:08.870
(AUDIO: Venezuelans in Colombia targeted by
threatening pamphlets.

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:09.870
- CNN Español)

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:13.880
Of course, this is by no means the majority,
and if Latin American societies have shown

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:19.080
anything, it’s a huge solidarity to welcome
thousands and thousands, hundreds of thousands

00:14:19.080 --> 00:14:23.060
of Venezuelans who’ve been forced to flee
their country.

00:14:23.060 --> 00:14:28.320
Yes, criminals must be prosecuted but it wouldn’t
be fair for the innocent to pay for the sins

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:30.380
of sinners, would it?

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:35.510
What’s more, these type of xenophobic outbreaks
favor the Venezuelan narco-dictatorship as

00:14:35.510 --> 00:14:39.930
they reduce the pressure on the regime itself.

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:46.340
Anyway, this is the reality of violence in
Venezuela and all these factors explain why

00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:48.490
this country has become a true nightmare.

00:14:48.490 --> 00:14:49.610
But now it’s your turn:

00:14:49.610 --> 00:14:53.630
Can violence be fought in a country ruled
by people who order criminals to kill?

00:14:53.630 --> 00:14:57.350
Should Venezuelans abroad face their violent
compatriots in order to end xenophobia?

00:14:57.350 --> 00:15:01.750
And what’s even more important, do you think
this hateful behavior against all of them

00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:03.210
is justified?

00:15:03.210 --> 00:15:04.970
We ask the questions, you provide the answers.

00:15:04.970 --> 00:15:08.910
So I really hope you enjoyed this video, please
hit like if you did, and don’t forget to

00:15:08.910 --> 00:15:10.560
subscribe for brand new videos.

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:13.630
Don’t forget to check out our friends at
the Reconsider Media Podcast - they provided

00:15:13.630 --> 00:15:16.000
the vocals in this episode that were not mine.

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:19.680
Also, this channel is possible because of
Patreon, and our patrons on that platform.

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:23.300
Please consider joining them and supporting
our mission of providing independent political

00:15:23.300 --> 00:15:24.300
coverage.

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:34.140
And as always, I’ll see you in the next
video.

